  The pattern language has no way to say what's in a pattern

  gistp:SparqlPattern is defined as "a graph of triples, each of which contains
  an instance of a SparqlVariable" — but there is no property anywhere in the
  ontology relating a pattern to its triples. The complete property inventory is
  oneOf, iriTemplate, variableText, instructionText, containedText. The lone
  example individual, gistp:_Thing_sample_1, is a variable, not a member of any
  pattern.

  So the deterministic compiler's very first question — "which triples am I
  compiling?" — is unanswerable from the vocabulary. I'd put this above the
  backlog's gistp:Rule item: a Rule that points at a match pattern and a
  construct pattern buys nothing while a pattern has no extension.

  The corollary is that the design's best idea has a cost. Making a pattern
  simultaneously valid domain data and a placeholder is genuinely nice — a
  reasoner can check it, unlike a SPIN blob. But it means :_IDType_1 
  gistp:variableText "?x" and :_IDType_1 a gist:IDType sit in the same graph,
  and only the second is payload. convert_to_sparql.rq doesn't strip the first.

  Named graph per pattern dissolves both problems at once: pattern IRI is the
  graph IRI, payload lives inside it, metadata stays in the default graph, zero
  new vocabulary, and GRAPH ?pattern { ... } gives you membership for free.
  That's my recommendation, though it does commit you to a quad store.

  Two things compile more cleanly than the notes suggest

  oneOf → VALUES, unchanged in both roles. It constrains as L; as R, a CONSTRUCT
  with VALUES in the WHERE emits one copy of the template per value — the
  coproduct behavior your design notes want, with no special-casing. Nice when a
  construct falls out of the semantics rather than being bolted on.

  iriTemplate → BIND(IRI(CONCAT(...)) AS ?x) in the WHERE. Minting is inherently
  construct-side, and that's its exact SPARQL form. (Mustache.jl is already a
  dep but won't help — RFC 6570 is single-brace with a {+var} operator, Mustache
  is double-brace with neither. A ~20-line Level-1/2 expander is less work than
