⏺ SPO vs DPO — the question is malformed, and that's good news

  DPO's dangling condition is a well-formedness constraint: delete a node while
  edges still point at it and the result isn't a graph. In RDF that's vacuous.
  Triples are edges whose endpoints are IRIs, and an IRI exists independently of
  any triple. Delete :x a :Person while :y :knows :x survives and you have a
  perfectly well-formed RDF graph. Nothing dangles because there's no node
  object to dangle from.

  So "I want DPO for RDF safety" is a category error as usually stated. What
  people actually want is a guard — don't fire if the node is still referenced.
  That's referential-integrity policy, not categorical necessity. Which is a
  much better position to be in: it's per-rule rather than global, and it's
  cheap (MINUS { ?other ?p ?x . FILTER(?other != ?x) }). CLAUDE.md's "silent
  orphans" worry is real, but it's a modeling concern, not a soundness one.

  DPO's actual payoff is elsewhere: local Church-Rosser, the parallelism
  theorem, critical-pair analysis. Those need the span as a real object — which
  is thread three.

  f(G) vs G ∪ f(G) — I think this is the sharpest of the four

  Two genuinely different operators, and conflating them is the classic error:

  - f(G) — the CONSTRUCT result alone. Composes as f∘g. Not monotone closure; a
  transformation, typically leaving the input vocabulary behind. That's the
  point when you're migrating.
  - G ∪ f(G) — inflationary. What a reasoner does. Iterating reaches a least
  fixpoint by Knaster–Tarski, but it doesn't compose freely — the setting is a
  lattice, not a category of graphs and morphisms.

  This pushes me toward three modes, not two: construct (pure, composable),
  assert (monotone, iterate to fixpoint), rewrite (DELETE/INSERT, stateful).
  CLAUDE.md's instinct to make mode explicit is right; I'd just split its
  CONSTRUCT-only case in half, because those two halves have opposite algebra.

  And the payoff: construct and assert compile to the same SPARQL text. The
  difference is entirely in the harness — does the result land in a fresh graph
  or get unioned back. One compiler, three drivers. assert is also precisely the
  mode you hand to a real reasoner rather than implementing, exactly as
  CLAUDE.md warns.

  One subtlety: f∘g dynamically is just "run g then f." Composing them
  statically — fusing R_g into L_f to get one rule — is a pushout, and needs the
  span objects.

  Does I need to be real? Enough to run, not enough to reason

  Shared-variable identity is sufficient for execution: per match of L you need
  the substitution that instantiates R, and that's what it gives you. SPARQL
  does no more than this.

  It's insufficient for analysis, because I isn't a set of shared nodes — it's a
  pair of morphisms carrying which triples are preserved. Take:

  L = { ?x a :A .  ?x :p ?y }        R = { ?x a :B .  ?x :p ?y }

  Shared variables are ?x, ?y either way. But is ?x :p ?y preserved (in I) or
  deleted and reasserted (not in I)? Extensionally identical. For parallel
  independence and critical pairs, completely different — two rules touching :p
  conflict if either deletes it, and don't if both preserve it.

  My recommendation: treat structural L ∩ R as the derived, maximal I, make it
  the default, and keep the door open to stating I explicitly for the rare
  reassert case. Don't build that machinery now, just don't design it out. It
  also hands you the backlog's validation query free: rules whose derived I is 
  empty are total replacements, and almost certainly bugs.

  Catlab — detour, but for a specific reason

  A Catlab C-Set is a functor from a fixed, finitely-presented schema category
  to Set: typed and closed. RDF is untyped, open-world, and self-describing,
  with the schema sitting in the same graph as the data. To use
  AlgebraicRewriting you'd first pin the graph to a fixed schema — discarding
  the exact property that makes patterns-as-RDF interesting.

  A faithful encoding does exist (RDF as a C-Set over objects Triple, Node with
  arrows s,p,o) and would give real DPO with real dangling conditions. But then
  the structure lives in Julia with no relation to your store and you own the
  marshalling both ways.

  Where Catlab is genuinely without peer is tradition 4 — functorial migration,
  Δ/Σ/Π. That's the entry point to take if the question becomes "migrate between
  ontologies provably." Not because you want DPO. I'd keep SPARQL as the trunk
  and treat Catlab as a branch gated on a migration requirement appearing.

  Footnote: the BGP restriction is load-bearing twice

  Your grounding fact has a sting. Graph homomorphism is NP-complete, and SPARQL
  BGP matching is NP-complete in combined complexity — harmless in practice
  since patterns are small. But adding OPTIONAL jumps evaluation to
  PSPACE-complete. So "SparqlPattern is deliberately just a BGP" is justified
  not only by safe L/R dual-use (the stated reason in your design notes) but
  independently by complexity. Two unrelated arguments landing on the same
  restriction is usually a sign the design is right.

