  Why the current code has to interleave them

  Look at build_pass_one! (src/build.jl:33): for each triple it either @evals a
  call to an existing function, or generates the function and pushes onto
  tl.futures. Compile and execute are decided per-triple.

  The futures queue exists because of world age. make_typed_object_prop evals a
  method definition, but the running build_pass_one! had its world age fixed at
  entry, so it cannot call what it just defined. Hence: queue it, and let
  build_pass_two! @eval $e it at top level where world age is escaped. The
  futures queue isn't a semantic requirement about forward references — it's a
  workaround for defining and calling in the same dynamic extent.

  Which means your split doesn't just tidy the design, it structurally dissolves
  the problem. If every declaration happens before any execution, there's
  nothing to defer. Forward references stop being special, futures goes away,
  and build_pass_two!'s per-triple @eval — currently the dominant cost, since it
  invokes the compiler once per statement — becomes a plain function call.

  The crux: signatures are currently inferred from data

  Here's what makes the split non-trivial. make_typed_object_prop derives its
  method signature by calling retrieve!(tl, s) and retrieve!(tl, o) — it reads
  the instance data to find subject and object types (src/build.jl:132-142). So
  the generated code depends on the A-Box, which is exactly why it can't be
  generated ahead of time.

  To separate the phases you have to move signature derivation to the schema:
  rdfs:domain and rdfs:range, falling back to RORB/Any where undeclared. That's
  more correct anyway — a property's domain and range are T-Box facts, not
  accidents of which triples you happened to load. And it's what makes "run
  once, then only execute" actually true: the generated code becomes a function
  of the ontology alone, so instance data never touches the compiler.

  Shape that falls out

  1. Analyze — pure, no eval. Walk triples, build a schema model: predicates,
  their domains/ranges, classes, subclass edges. Plain data structure.
  2. Generate — pure. Schema model → a single Expr. Inspectable, testable,
  diffable, and writable to a .jl file you can precompile.
  3. Execute — eval the block once (or include the generated file), then stream
  data through with ordinary calls.

  Phase 3 is where "subsequent calls only execute existing code" lives, and it's
  free once 1 and 2 are pure.

  The part I like: this is the same architecture as the pattern→SPARQL compiler.
  Analyze once, then swap backends — one emitting Julia methods, one emitting
  CONSTRUCT/DELETE…INSERT. That's CLAUDE.md's "Hybrid" bullet (two backends off
  the same RDF) arrived at from the opposite direction, which is some evidence
  it's the right factoring.

  One open question I'd want your read on: for properties with no declared
  domain/range, do you want the generic (::RORB, ::Any) method, or should phase
  1 collect observed types from a data scan and generate specialized methods
  from that? The second keeps today's dispatch precision but re-couples
  generation to data — though only to a sample, which is a weaker coupling than
  today's.

