  Your instinct was right, and the bug was concrete

  The smoking gun was two lines in src/rdf_type.jl:

  $nm(s::Unknown, o, tl::TraceLog = $tl) = store_local!($tl, o, s.uri)

  The body used the interpolated $tl, not the tl parameter — so a generated
  function accepted a TraceLog and then ignored it. Measured before the
  refactor:

  make_from_rdf(data2, tl3)   # fresh TraceLog, new data
  tl3.ldict 0 -> 0    (got nothing)
  tl1.ldict 4 -> 5    (the PREVIOUS run's tracelog absorbed it)

  I had predicted this would only bite when the argument was omitted. It was
  broader — explicit passing didn't help either.

  What changed

  Three phases, analyze → generate → install!/register!/run_data!. Analyze and
  generate are pure; install! holds the only Core.eval in the pipeline.

  futures and build_pass_two! are gone. They were a world-age workaround — code
  generated and called in the same dynamic extent is invisible to the running
  function. Generating everything before executing anything removes the need to
  defer; one invokelatest at the dispatch point replaces a full @eval per
  triple. build.jl went from 278 lines to 36.

  Signature derivation moved from instance data (retrieve! on the A-Box) to the
  schema, so generated code is a function of the ontology alone. generate skips
  anything already defined — that's literally "compile once."

  Proof

  Test suite 17 → 53, all green. The four compile-once tests were written first
  and shown failing on the old code, then passing.

  On the real ontology (gistAcct3.0.0.ttl, 833 triples), against master at
  68ded5c in a clean worktree:

  ┌─────────────────────────┬────────────────┬───────────────────┐
  │                         │     master     │     refactor      │
  ├─────────────────────────┼────────────────┼───────────────────┤
  │ run 1 (compile+execute) │ threw, ldict=0 │ 2.76s, ldict=329  │
  ├─────────────────────────┼────────────────┼───────────────────┤
  │ run 2 (fresh TraceLog)  │ threw, ldict=0 │ 0.008s, ldict=329 │
  ├─────────────────────────┼────────────────┼───────────────────┤
  │ leak into run 1's log   │ —              │ none              │
  └─────────────────────────┴────────────────┴───────────────────┘

  Run 2 is 350× faster with an identical result in its own TraceLog. There's no
  speedup number against master because master couldn't load the file at all.

  Three pre-existing bugs I had to fix to get there

  - src/rdf.jl:191 — norm(s::ResourceURI) = Resource(string(s)). string on a
  Serd term renders the constructor call, so every full IRI <http://…> became
  the literal text ResourceURI("http://…"). Same catch-all mangled blank nodes
  into Resource("Blank(\"b1\")"). Only prefixed names were ever tested, so
  neither was noticed.
  - makeqname aborting analysis — it throws on an unregistered prefix (there's a
  test asserting that). One bare IRI killed the whole file; unnameable terms
  are now skipped and recorded.
  - Namespace drift — your uncommitted .../ns/ontologies/gist/ (plural) is a
  typo; the singular has 395,405 occurrences across gistPatterns and
  SyneticSemantics and zero for the plural. Both spellings are now registered,
  so legacy ontologies.semanticarts.com data still resolves and no test needed
  changing.

  Not fixed — flagging rather than expanding scope

  24 of 833 gistAcct triples still fail to execute. Almost all are
  rdfs_subClassOf doing push!(s.super, o) where retrieve! handed it a generated
  type rather than an instance, so .super resolves to Any. It's pre-existing and
  unrelated to the split — master never got far enough to hit it. The subclass
  data is captured correctly in m.classes[…].supers regardless, which arguably
  makes that legacy mutation path redundant. Happy to clean it up if you want.

  Also unfixed: makeqname produces ex_is-categorized-by for hyphenated local
  names, which is not a legal Julia identifier. It works today only because
  nothing calls it by literal syntax — it would break the moment generate writes
  to a .jl file.

  The last point matters for where this is heading: generate returns an ordinary
  Expr, so emitting a precompilable file is now a small step, and the same
  SchemaModel is what the pattern→SPARQL backend would consume.

