  The gap this closes

  The pattern language couldn't say which triples belong to a pattern, nor which
  L pairs with which R. Until both existed there was nothing deterministic to
  compile.

  Membership is now by named graph — a pattern's IRI is its graph IRI. No
  membership vocabulary needed, and variable descriptions stay in the default
  graph, so a compiler emits GRAPH <pattern> { ?s ?p ?o } as a BGP with nothing
  to filter. Turtle can't express that, so instances are TriG.

  Vocabulary added: gistp:Rule, hasMatchPattern, hasConstructPattern,
  rewriteMode with Construct / Assert / Rewrite. I is deliberately not stated —
  it's derived as L ∩ R, which is literal RDF identity because variables are
  persistent individuals.

  Fixes along the way: the iriTemplate example used <id> where RFC 6570 wants
  {id}; the ontology IRI said gistPatterningDefinitions while @base said
  gistPatternDefinitions; oneOf had no domain; catalog-v001.xml wasn't
  well-formed XML. process_instances_example.ttl is replaced — it was invalid
  Turtle parsing to zero triples.

