  What I is in Person→Employee

  The two graphs, as authored:

  turtle
  :PersonToEmployee_L {                          # 4 triples
      :_Person_1 a gist:Person ;
                 gist:isIdentifiedBy :_ID_1 .
      :_ID_1     a gist:ID ;
                 gist:containedText "?idText"^^gistp:var .
  }
  :PersonToEmployee_R {                          # 2 triples
      :_Person_1 a ex:Employee ;
                 ex:employeeNumber "?idText"^^gistp:var .
  }

  The triple-level intersection is empty. Not one triple appears in both. What L
  and R share is a single node: :_Person_1.

  So I is the discrete graph on {:_Person_1} — one node, no edges. That's a
  perfectly good graph and a perfectly good span; I is the interface, and here
  the interface is bare identity: "the thing that was a Person is the same thing
  that is now an Employee."

  Now run it as Rewrite and see what that buys you:

  - L ∖ I = all four triples → deleted
  - R ∖ I = both triples → inserted
  - Preserved = the node, and nothing else

  Which means the person stops being a gist:Person, and :_ID_1 is left stranded
  with no properties and no referent. As a Rewrite rule, this would be a bug —
  and it's the dangling condition showing up in your own worked example rather
  than in a textbook. As the Construct rule it actually declares, it's fine,
  because nothing is deleted.

  The practical lesson worth writing into the ontology: I is authored by 
  repetition. Whatever you want preserved, you write in both L and R. If the
  intent were "keep the person, replace the gist ID structure with a flat
  number," R would repeat :_Person_1 a gist:Person, and I would become
  {:_Person_1, :_Person_1 a gist:Person}. That's also precisely why the mode has
  to be explicit rather than inferred: repetition determines I, and the mode
  determines what's done with L ∖ I. Two independent decisions.

  One thing that should reassure you: ?idText is shared between L and R but it
  is not a node in I. It's a value carried by the matching morphism. That's not
  a wart in your design — it's the node/edge vs. attribute split from attributed
  graph transformation, where labels are handled by a separate algebra
  precisely because values aren't nodes. Your two variable mechanisms mirror a
  real distinction in the theory. The implementation still needs the
  use-before-def check, but the modelling is sound.

  Getting legacy rules in

  Chat is the demo. This is the actual adoption problem, and it splits by
  tractability.

  Start by not migrating. The highest-leverage move is R2RML (W3C Rec) plus
  Ontop, which gives you a virtual knowledge graph: you write mappings, and
  Ontop rewrites SPARQL into SQL against the live relational database. No data
  movement, no big-bang cutover, no freeze. Your patterns run against the legacy
  system as it stands. This removes the single biggest risk that kills
  data-centric projects. The catch is that virtual graphs are read-only —
  Construct and Assert work, Rewrite doesn't. Another reason to ship those two
  first.

  Then harvest what's already declarative. In rough order of yield per hour:

  - Views and materialized views — a view is a declarative rule. It's the
  closest thing in the relational world to a CONSTRUCT pattern, and the mapping
  is nearly mechanical.
  - Check constraints and FKs → SHACL shapes and object properties. Free
  structure.
  - Existing rules engines (Drools, ILOG) — already when…then; a DRL left-hand
  side is a pattern match over facts, which is L. Low impedance.
  - Triggers and stored procedures — declarative intent wrapped in imperative
  code. Mixed.

  Imperative application code is the hard tier, and the trick is verification, 
  not extraction. You will not mechanically lift rules out of 200k lines of
  Java. But you don't need the extraction to be right — you need to be able to
  check it. If the legacy system runs, you have an oracle.

  So: use program slicing to find the code computing a given output field,
  extract a candidate rule with LLM assistance, compile it, run it over
  production data, and diff its output against what legacy produces for the same
  inputs. Disagreements are either extraction bugs or undocumented legacy
  behaviour — and both are valuable findings. This is characterization testing,
  and it's how successful mainframe migrations actually get done. Your
  architecture suits it unusually well because Construct-mode rules are small,
  isolated and side-effect-free: you can validate one rule at a time against
  real data.

  A third authoring path you haven't mentioned: mine the rules from the data.
  Once the graph exists — even virtually — tools like AMIE and AnyBURL mine Horn
  rules from RDF with confidence scores, and they emit exactly the shape you
  already use. That gives you candidate patterns statistically, which humans or
  an LLM then curate. It's especially good at surfacing rules nobody documented,
  and at catching where the legacy code and the actual data disagree.

  Where migrations actually go wrong, so you can budget for it:

  - Legacy rules are frequently contradictory or wrong, and translation surfaces
  it. The schedule killer is adjudication, not conversion — someone has to
  decide what the rule should be, and that's a business decision.
  - A great deal of "business logic" is process, not derivation: sequencing,
  approvals, exceptions, compensation. That's your control-layer gap, and it's
  where the BPMN thread and this thread meet.
  - Encoded assumptions — nulls, sentinel dates, currency rounding, -1 meaning
  "unknown" — don't survive naive translation and don't announce themselves.
  - The distribution is brutal: ~80% of rules are simple derivations you'll
  convert quickly, and the remaining 20% carry most of the business value and
  most of the difficulty.

  The strangler-fig shape follows from all of that: virtualize, harvest the
  declarative, characterize the imperative, run new rules alongside legacy while
  diffing, and cut over one rule at a time.

