  What copying L into R actually compiles to

  If R ⊇ L, then I = L, so L ∖ I = ∅:

  sparql
  DELETE { }                  # empty — nothing to delete
  INSERT { ?p a ex:Employee . ?p ex:employeeNumber ?idText }
  WHERE  { ... L ... }

  An empty DELETE clause. That's Assert with extra steps — and in Assert you
  don't repeat L at all, because the result is G ∪ f(G). The original graph is
  preserved by the mode, not by the pattern. You author only the new triples.

  So: mechanically yes, it works. Practically, it's the wrong tool, and it
  carries a nasty failure mode. Repeated triples must stay byte-identical in
  both graphs forever. Add a triple to L six months from now to tighten the
  match, forget to mirror it in R, and that triple silently moves from I to L ∖
  I — and the rule starts deleting data. Silent, delayed, and caused by an edit
  that looks locally correct.

  The same syntax means three different things

  This is why the mode has to be explicit. R ⊇ L reads differently in each:

  ┌───────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │   mode    │                 what repeating L in R means                  │
  ├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Construct │ carry the input forward — f(G) is the entire output, so      │
  │           │ anything you want in the result must be in R                 │
  ├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Assert    │ redundant — G is already preserved by the union              │
  ├───────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Rewrite   │ preserve — and if you repeat all of L, it degenerates to     │
  │           │ Assert                                                       │
  └───────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Repetition determines I. The mode determines what's done with L ∖ I. Two
  orthogonal decisions, which is exactly why inferring the mode from which
  triples happen to repeat would be wrong.

  The deeper point about your example

  "An Employee is still a Person, and whatever IDs they have remain" — that's
  not a rewrite at all. Nothing becomes false. It's purely additive, so Assert
  is the right mode and the question dissolves.

  Reclassification-by-deletion is a relational instinct: you'd UPDATE a
  discriminator column, because a row has one value per column. RDF has no such
  constraint. "Becomes an Employee" means gains a type, and the old type stays
  true. If you also declare ex:Employee rdfs:subClassOf gist:Person, the person
  typing comes back by entailment anyway.

  When Rewrite genuinely earns its place

  Reserve deletion for facts that are actually false now — where old and new
  cannot both be true:

  - Correction — a wrong birth date. You can't hold both.
  - Functional properties — two values is an OWL inconsistency, so deletion is
  forced.
  - Mutually exclusive state — :order :hasStatus :Submitted → :Approved. Keeping
  both is meaningless.
  - Retention and redaction — erasure requests, expiry.
  - Structural migration — replacing a modelling pattern where leaving the old
  one double-counts in queries.

  The third one is the interesting case: that's a token moving through a
  process, and it's where Rewrite stops being a data-cleanup mode and becomes
  the process mode. Which is exactly where the missing control layer starts to
  bite — a state transition needs a NAC so it fires once, and a strategy so the
  next transition knows to follow.

