⏺ Recommendation

  Make the template the role marker, bind slots by RDF identity, and let SPARQL 
  do the expansion.

  1. A template declares a minted node

  Presence of gistp:iriTemplate means "this variable is constructed, not
  matched." No new property needed — the template itself is the role marker. The
  compiler then enforces:

  ▎ A variable with a template must not be bound by L.

  That resolves the ambiguity and, usefully, condemns the case that started
  this: :_Person_1 is matched by L and carries :_Employee_{person_id}. Under
  this rule that's an error — correctly, because the template there is dead.
  PersonToEmployee reuses the matched IRI and never mints. Fix is to delete the
  template from the example, and add a separate minting example.

  2. Slots bind explicitly, never by name matching

  turtle
  :_Employee_1 a ex:Employee , gistp:SparqlVariable ;
      gistp:variableText "?_Employee_1" ;
      gistp:iriTemplate "http://example.org/hr/employee/{id}" ;
      gistp:hasSlot [ gistp:slotName "id" ; gistp:slotValue :_ID_1 ] .

  Three new terms: hasSlot, slotName, slotValue. Binding is RDF identity, so
  it's SHACL-checkable and reuses the existing use-before-def machinery. I
  deliberately rejected the obvious cheaper option — {person_id} implicitly
  binding ?person_id — because that's a second string-matching mechanism, and we
  already have one footgun of exactly that shape in literal-position variables.

  Round 1 restriction: a slot value must be bound by L, not by another minted
  variable. That sidesteps BIND ordering entirely; chained minting can come
  later with a topological sort.

  3. Templates expand to absolute IRIs

  This is a breaking change to the property's stated meaning — it currently says
  "the local part of a IRI," with examples resolving against an implicit
  prefix. That's unimplementable as written, and in example_rule.trig the empty
  prefix is the rules namespace, so expanding it would mint employees into
  http://example.org/rules/. Almost certainly not what anyone wants.

  Requiring absolute makes the minting namespace explicit and auditable at the
  point of use, which matters more than brevity when these are machine-authored
  anyway. No mintBase, no resolution logic.

  4. SPARQL does the expansion

  RFC 6570 Level 1 is exactly ENCODE_FOR_URI:

  sparql
  WHERE {
    ?_Person_1 a gist:Person ; gist:isIdentifiedBy ?_ID_1 .
    ?_ID_1 gist:containedText ?idText .
    BIND(IRI(CONCAT("http://example.org/hr/employee/",
                    ENCODE_FOR_URI(STR(?idText)))) AS ?_Employee_1)
  }

  ENCODE_FOR_URI percent-encodes everything outside RFC 3986's unreserved set —
  character-for-character what Level 1 specifies. So there's no template engine
  in Julia at all; the compiler emits BIND and the store expands. Consistent
  with the rest of the design: SPARQL matches, and here it mints too.

  Level 2 ({+var}) refused in round 1 with a clear message. STR() alone would be
  more permissive than Level 2 — it doesn't encode spaces or <> — and shipping
  an approximation labelled "RFC 6570" is worse than shipping half of it
  honestly.

