⏺ What I want to do: not slugging. Not yet.

  Looking at it properly turned up something that outranks it.

  The live bug

  Two distinct people can already collapse into one minted node, today, with
  plain Level 1 encoding:

  "x_y" + "z"   ->  http://ex.org/x_y_z
  "x"   + "y_z" ->  http://ex.org/x_y_z     ← same IRI, different data

  In RDF, IRI identity is identity. That isn't an ugly-URL problem, it's a
  silent data merge — two employees become one employee, and nothing anywhere
  complains. Slugging would make this worse, but it didn't cause it.

  The cause is that ENCODE_FOR_URI deliberately leaves - . _ ~ alone, so any
  template separator drawn from the unreserved set is ambiguous with the same
  character appearing inside a value.

  And the fix is pleasingly cheap — use a separator from outside the unreserved 
  set:

  "x/y" + "z"   ->  http://ex.org/x%2Fy/z
  "x"   + "y/z" ->  http://ex.org/x/y%2Fz     ← distinct, because values get %2F

  So here's what I want to build, in order

  1. A static separator lint. If a multi-slot template's literal text between
  two slots is entirely unreserved characters, refuse it and say why,
  recommending /. Free, no runtime cost, catches the whole class at compile
  time.

  2. A runtime collision check. The backstop for what the lint can't see. It's
  nearly free because the rule's own WHERE clause already binds both the slot
  values and the minted IRI — group by the minted IRI, and any group covering
  more than one distinct binding tuple is a collision. No extra triples
  persisted, runs at apply time, refuses the firing.

  3. Slugging — only after 1 and 2, and only as opt-in.

  And on slugging itself, a distinction worth making

  Your instinct and my worry are both right, about different inputs.

  Slug controlled vocabulary terms. Never slug data.

  Longitudinal Load → longitudinal_load is fine, because it's a curated term
  from a finite list — you can enumerate the vocabulary and check for collisions
  once, at authoring time. That's why your :_Aspect_longitudinal_load example
  reads well.

  E 9902/A is not that. It's free-text data you don't control, can't enumerate,
  and where E 9902/A and E-9902-A and e 9902 a all slug to the same thing.
  Slugging identity from data is how you merge two employees.

  The collision check makes that boundary empirical rather than a matter of
  exhortation — a rule that slugs data will trip it in production, on real
  values, rather than passing review and quietly corrupting the graph.

  The thing I'd actually reach for first

  For the readability want specifically: don't slug the IRI, put the readable 
  text in skos:prefLabel. R can already construct that today, zero new
  vocabulary:

  turtle
  :_Employee_1 skos:prefLabel "?idText"^^gistp:var .

  IRIs identify; labels label. That's the data-centric-correct split, and it's
  the one that doesn't trade identity safety for legibility. If you want
  debuggable output, this gets you it without any of the above being
  load-bearing.

  ---
  My recommendation: let me build 1 and 2 now — they close a real hole and
  they're small. Then decide on slugging once you can see whether the collision
  check ever fires on your actual data. If you'd rather I just do all three, say
  so and I'll build slugging with the check mandatory whenever it's enabled.

